# Crisis Scenario Brief: El Niño-Driven Drought Response

## Executive Summary
An ongoing severe El Niño cycle has severely reduced monsoon rainfall, resulting in a critical 42% deficit in total regional water reserves. Major agricultural zones face catastrophic crop failures, while metropolitan areas are projecting a complete depletion of municipal reservoirs within 60 days. An Inter-Agency Crisis Task Force has been convened to manage immediate water allocation and implement emergency infrastructure measures.
Technical Data & Environmental Metrics
Reservoir Capacity: Operating at 18% usable capacity (Critical Baseline: 25%).

Groundwater Depletion: Depleting at an accelerated rate of 1.2 meters per week due to unmonitored agricultural extraction.

Urban Supply Deficit: Current supply provides 65 Gallons Per Capita per Day (GPCD) against a standard requirement of 130 GPCD.

Agricultural Impact: 340,000 hectares of arable land affected; major crop yield loss projected at 60–75% for the current season.

Immediate Task Force Objectives
Water Rationing Framework: Establish immediate allocation percentages across Agricultural, Municipal, and Industrial sectors.

Emergency Infrastructure: Evaluate rapid-deployment options including mobile desalination units, emergency pipelines, and deep-aquifer drilling.

Fiscal Allocation: Authorize emergency bond drawdowns to finance emergency relief logistics while maintaining debt stability.
